Frequency,\" and their song \"Super-Sprøde\" was featured in the game's sequel, \"Amplitude.\" Freezepop won Best New Band in 2001 at the American Synthpop Awards and Best US Band in 2002.\r\n\r\nThe Other Sean T. Drinkwater is a clone of the original who was too busy with his first synthpop group [url=https://www.discogs.com/artist/321065-Lifestyle-3]Lifestyle[/url], his solo work as [a330416], and his heavy metal group Godclaw.\r\n\r\nIn addition to singing, Liz Enthusiasm designs the art for the band's albums, designed their website and the website of Sean's other band, Lifestyle, and has animated music videos for some of their songs.\r\n\r\nThe Duke of Pannekoeken was previously known as The Duke of Candied Apples and, before that, the Duke of Belgian Waffles. He is also the man behind [a203671] and was a member of the alternative rock group [a178462]. In early June 2009, The Duke announced he was leaving the band.\r\n\r\nAfter The Duke's departure, [a1261906] (aka Bananas-Foster) was officially added to the lineup.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:67830","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":null,"members":[{"active":false,"id":458496,"name":"Kasson Crooker"},{"active":true,"id":605267,"name":"Sean T. Drinkwater"},{"active":true,"id":650348,"name":"Jussi Gamache"},{"active":true,"id":1261906,"name":"Seth Damascus-Kennedy"},{"active":true,"id":1922453,"name":"Ashley Holtgraver"}],"namevariations":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/67830-Freezepop","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.freezepop.net","https://www.facebook.com/freezepopband","https://www.instagram.com/freezepopband","https://twitter.com/freezepopband","https://freezepop.bandcamp.com","https://www.youtube.com/@freezepop","https://www.twitch.tv/freezepopband","https://linktr.ee/freezepopband","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freezepop"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/730/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"730","image":"https://i.discogs.com/fLk9QcPD_ZkFCxguQSi2k46N814lDjiCrBcTnTyIbnI/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:287/w:300/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTczMC0x/MDc0NTI4NjExLmdp/Zg.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/LsiqF8CAc2r8LHiWwOkdX89CTAENp3hHMgP2RptWp2E/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTczMC0x/MDc0NTI4NjExLmdp/Zg.jpeg","profile":"","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:730","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":17682,"name":"DJ Decent"},{"id":228285,"name":"Mr. Butch"},{"id":1257232,"name":"Hollisinate"}],"groups":[{"active":true,"id":10459,"name":"Tigerhook Corp."},{"active":true,"id":14971,"name":"Universal Agents"},{"active":true,"id":3422552,"name":"The Black 80s"}],"members":null,"namevariations":["H. Monroe","H. P. Monroe","H.P. Monroe","H.P.Monroe","Hollies P. Monroe","Hollis Monroe","Hollis P","Hollis P Monroe","Hollis P.","Hollis.P.Monroe","Hollister P Monroe","Monroe, Hollis P"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/730-Hollis-P-Monroe","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.myspace.com/hollispmonroemusic","http://www.tigerhook.com","http://www.pmonroe.com"]}},"/api/discogs/label/1422002/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1422002","image":"","imageThumb":"","profile":"","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:1422002","relations":{"parentLabel":null,"sublabels":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/1422002-Whities-Records","type":"label","urls":[]}},"/api/discogs/label/2294/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"2294","image":"https://i.discogs.com/sSHvafK_7vhXYlIb4uG3obiCcc90uaIuTpImRS8a5LU/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:554/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTIyOTQt/MTYxNTE5Njk3OS03/NTM4LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/ksyaSCotx4vMbM9VFhNlEp_2Wcmxe4YmobVqw2IOd3A/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTIyOTQt/MTYxNTE5Njk3OS03/NTM4LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Multinational label.\nLabel Code: [b]LC 0299 / LC 00299[/b]\n[b]Please note, many Parlophone issues also carry an EMI logo. This is not a label and EMI should not be added as a label, it instead indicates that Parlophone was part of the EMI Group.[/b]\n\nParlophone is an anglicization of the German [l=Parlophon] label, registered as a trademark for talking machines in 1902 by Max Straus and Heinrich Zuntz of [b]Salon Kinematograph Co. GmbH[/b], later merged into [l=Carl Lindström A.-G.]. The first Parlophon records are pressed in 1910. \n\nBy 1923, Parlophone proper appears in London administered by English Lindström branch known as [l311921], pressed in London and sold domestically. This marks the return of the German conglomerate to the British market since 1915, when their Hertford record plant had been seized and soon acquired by [l97841], who leased it to Lindström to make Parlophone records. Early on, its catalogue greatly relied on imported recordings originally made for Lindström's own imprints [l=Beka], [l=Parlophon], [l=Odeon] (including Argentine tango recordings) and their American partner [l=Okeh] alongside a slowly growing repertoire of new British recordings and a small number of pre-war recordings. After World War II, Parlophone issued dozens of [l=Cetra] recordings, including new popular hits and classical pieces, some of which were recorded before the war. By 1926 extended operations extended to press for export in Australia (catalogue numbers prefixed A) until the Australian factory [l=Parlophone Co. Ltd.] was created somewhere the next year. These companies were subsequently taken over by [l97841] in 1927, following its acquisition of [l275192] in 1926, thus becoming a constituent part of [l293204] (EMI) when that company merged with [l253617] in 1931.\n\nThe label's first A&R was [a=Oscar Carl Preuss], producer and engineer who had previously worked for [l=Carl Lindström A.-G.] In 1955, he was succeded by [a=George Martin], who introduced notable comic talent soon after. The label flourished in the 1960s as it pivoted away from classical and novelty issues into vocal pop with singers like [a=Adam Faith] and [a=Matt Munro], followed by beat groups [a=The Beatles], [a=The Hollies], [a=Gerry & The Pacemakers], [a=Billy J. Kramer & The Dakotas] and singer [a=Cilla Black] who turned the label's status around from EMI's \"dumping ground\" to their dominant pop imprint.\nAs a result of its subsequent decline and profile, the label was earmarked for phasing out in 1973 in favour of [l=EMI] (the label), however the Parlophone imprint continued to be used sporadically by registered trademark owner [l=EMI Records Ltd.] and affiliated companies in certain markets and for some reissues. It was brought back in 1980 for most European markets.\n\nIn September 2012 following the acquisition of [l1330982]'s US and Asian interests by [l=Universal Music Group], Parlophone and the former EMI Records Ltd. were merged as the [l806790] (PLG Holdco Ltd.) and spun off to be sold. \n\nThey were sold to [l=Warner Music Group] in March 2013.\nThe Parlophone label is now owned by [l=Parlophone Records Ltd.], the re-named EMI Records Ltd., itself a subsidiary of [l=Warner Music International].\n\nThe Beatles were not part of the Parlophone Label Group and their catalogue was acquired by [l=Universal].\n\n[b]This label contains releases on multinational markets. Do NOT create imprints for different countries. Cover that information on new releases with the country field and with notes about the release (releasing company, ℗&© note, mechanical rights etc.).[/b]","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:2294","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":563997,"name":"Parlophone Records Ltd."},"sublabels":[{"id":829416,"name":"50"},{"id":2579467,"name":"A Flavour Of The Label"},{"id":510937,"name":"Bethlehem Series"},{"id":818232,"name":"Black And White Rhythm Style Series"},{"id":731156,"name":"Boogie Woogie Series"},{"id":1337739,"name":"British Artists' Rhythm Style Series"},{"id":1412,"name":"Credence"},{"id":944424,"name":"Further Listening"},{